# mgtrade.msg - "Lucky" Harith, the caravan trader outside the gate (mgtrade.ssl).
# 100-109 look and examine, 110-119 floating lines, 200+ conversation.
# One entry per line; no braces in comment lines.

{100}{}{You see a caravan trader.}
{101}{}{A sun-cured man with a ledger in his coat and the easy patience of someone who has outlived every partner he ever had.}

{110}{}{Easy, girl. Easy.}
{111}{}{Water, then north.}
{112}{}{Guns and iron! Fair prices, mostly.}
{113}{}{Luck's holding.}

# Greetings: 200 first, 201 later, 202 bomb disarmed.
{200}{}{Lucky Harith. Guns and iron, two brahmin and all my own fingers. Buying or selling? I'm gone when the water skins are full.}
{201}{}{Still alive, I see. Good. Dead men haggle badly. Buying or selling?}
{202}{}{Word is you killed the bomb. Shame. I had a nice trade going in 'last chance' shotgun shells. Buying or selling?}

{210}{}{Show me what you carry.}
{211}{}{Why do they call you Lucky?}
{212}{}{What's the road like?}
{213}{}{Why camp outside the wall?}
{214}{}{Can you carry a letter north?}
{215}{}{Who's the man at your fire?}
{216}{}{Safe roads, Harith.}
{217}{}{Boom sticks! Trade?}
{218}{}{Paper for girl's mama. You take.}
{219}{}{Bye bye, cow man.}
{220}{}{What became of that letter?}

{230}{}{Eleven years on the roads. Three partners, two wives and a guard dog. I'm the only one of us who never got shot. Luck's a fixed supply. I just stand next to people who are running out.}
{231}{}{Raiders on the Klamath trail. They let the first wagon by and take the second, so I travel alone and call myself the first wagon. If you walk east, walk early and light no fire.}
# Why outside: 232 bomb live, 233 bomb disarmed.
{232}{}{In there: a bomb, a preacher and a saloon keeper who writes down what you say. Out here: only everything else. I've done the sums.}
{233}{}{Habit. The bomb's dead, they tell me. The saloon keeper with the notebook isn't.}
{234}{}{Clean suit, clean shoes, warming his hands at my fire like he paid for the wood. He keeps looking at the town, then at his watch. I've seen that look on men waiting for a hanging.}
{235}{}{In the saddlebag, between the shells and the jerky. It gets there if I do. That's the whole postal service.}

# Postage: 240 the player holds the letter, 241 he sold it to the trader, 242 the paper is lost.
{240}{}{Mail's thirty. Paper weighs nothing, but the men who shoot at me don't ask what I'm hauling.}
{241}{}{Sealed, 'West family'... I bought that off you in a pile of other things. You want it delivered and not resold? Then it's mail, and mail's thirty.}
{242}{}{A letter you haven't got. That's the lightest cargo I was ever offered. Give me the name and the place and I'll say the words myself. Same thirty. Words get shot at too.}
{243}{}{Thirty. Here.}
{244}{}{Ten. You're going that way anyway.}
{245}{}{A girl alone, writing home. Carry it for the luck.}
{246}{}{I'll think about it.}
{250}{}{[The money goes in one pocket, the message in another.] It gets there if I do. If I don't, she'll have worse news than a late letter.}
{251}{}{...I am going that way. Ten, and you never tell another trader what I took. Done.}
{252}{}{[He looks at the wall for a while.] Luck's a fixed supply, and I've been spending other people's for years. All right. This one rides free.}
{253}{}{You're short. I don't carry on credit. The last man I trusted is why I own two brahmin and not three.}

# INT 3 and below.
{260}{}{[He takes this in.] Mail. You want mail carried. Thirty coins, friend. Three piles of ten. Show me.}
{261}{}{Me pay. [Give him $30.]}
{262}{}{No. Me keep.}
{263}{}{There. It rides with me. You did a kind thing just now. Don't let it worry you.}
{264}{}{That isn't thirty. Come back when the piles are bigger.}

{270}{}{Anything else? The brahmin are getting ideas about leaving.}
{271}{}{One more question.}
{272}{}{This end kills. That end you hold. Good. You're already ahead of my last partner.}
{273}{}{More boom sticks!}
